<GuideLayout slug="tree-removal-after-hurricane">
  <p class="note">
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> after a hurricane, keep everyone away from damaged trees and anything touching power
    lines, photograph the damage for your insurer, and have the tree removed by a professional with the right equipment.
    Do not climb or use a chainsaw on a tree under tension, on a structure or near lines.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Safety first</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If anyone is hurt or in danger, call 911.</li>
    <li>Assume every downed line is live and stay far away. Call your power company (in most of South Florida, FPL).</li>
    <li>Keep people and pets away from hanging limbs and leaning trees. They can fall without warning.</li>
    <li>Do not stand under a tree that is on a roof, and do not enter rooms below the damage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Document before anything is moved</h2>
  <p>
    Photograph the tree, the damage and the surroundings from several angles. Policies usually cover removal when a tree
    falls on a covered structure, such as the house or a fence, and often not when a tree falls in the yard without
    damage. Check your policy and keep every invoice.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Protect the property</h2>
  <p>
    Covering a damaged roof to stop water getting in is usually expected after a loss, and the cost is often
    reimbursable. Keep the receipts. Do not start permanent repairs before you have spoken to your insurer.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Choose who removes the tree</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Beware door-to-door crews.</strong> After a storm, outsiders often go street to street asking for cash up front.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for proof of license and insurance</strong> and get the scope and price in writing.</li>
    <li><strong>Be realistic about timing.</strong> After a major storm everyone needs help at once, and emergency crews go to the most urgent jobs first.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For a tree on a house, car or driveway, <a href="/emergency-tree-removal/">send an emergency request</a>. We flag it
    as urgent for independent local professionals, but we cannot promise an arrival time.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Debris and permits</h2>
  <p>
    Cities and counties often set rules for storm debris pickup, such as where to place it and how long collection
    lasts. Check your city's website. Many cities also allow emergency removal when a tree is an imminent danger, but may
    still ask for documentation afterward, so keep photos and the invoice. See <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">our
    permit guide</a> and the <a href="/tree-care-guides/do-i-need-a-permit-to-remove-a-tree/">permit question
    explained</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Before the next storm</h2>
  <p>
    Trees that are well structured, regularly pruned and free of dead wood survive storms better. University of Florida
    research shows that topping and heavy thinning (“hurricane cuts”) make trees weaker, not stronger. Get weak trees
    assessed before the season starts on June 1, and read <a href="/tree-care-guides/hurricane-tree-preparation/">how
    to prepare your trees for hurricane season</a>. For trimming that helps, see <a href="/tree-trimming/">tree
    trimming</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Cost and free options</h2>
  <p>
    Storm removal is usually paid work, but there are cases where you do not pay. See
    <a href="/free-tree-removal/">what is actually free</a> and <a href="/tree-care-guides/tree-removal-cost-south-florida/">how much
    tree removal costs in South Florida</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
